      "writtenOn": "2026-08-05T18:15:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Talking offline, we can achieve this on the GPU by adjusting how we draw the fill or the stroke step. Originally, I\u0027d proposed we draw the fill shape with a regular fill and no paint ID (basically blocking those pixels via the depth buffer), then follow it up with an inverse-filled stroke.\n\nHowever, that ends up using stencil inefficiently since the regular fill will write to the stencil then add a cover step to shift the fill rule into the depth buffer, and then we\u0027ll stencil the stroke and add a cover step for handling the inverse.\n\nIf we flip it around to draw the regular stroke into just the depth buffer, it won\u0027t use the stencil at all and will be a single step, and then it can be followed by the inverse-filled fill shape. This simplifies ordering a little bit since we don\u0027t need to worry about both draws using the stencil buffer (which would require adjusting the disjoint stencil index).\n\nHowever, unlike the regular-fill stroke+fill style, the order of the two draws is important.  We can force the depth-only draw to be first by advancing the CompressedPaintersOrder id of the inverse-fill\u0027s draw. An example of this is how the inner-fill draw logic works.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1596,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1607,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "9376256c561aac3d5083454982dc17b5d4b681bd",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a0f4f319_89db70e9",
        "filename": "src/gpu/graphite/Device.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 9
      },
      "lineNbr": 1607,
      "author": {
        "id": 3438316
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-07T15:01:04Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Implemented the proposed solution where we do a depth-only stroke first, then the inverse fill. Had to make adjustments to DrawList and DrawListLayer uniform gathering since I was proving an invalid paint id to get a depth-only draw, however it still assumed we were doing shading because of the render step\u0027s flags.\n\nI could have also created a separate TessellateStrokesRenderStep without `Flag::kPerformsShading` flag, or constructed an empty PipelineDataGatherer and passed that into the stroke draw call, however checking if the paint id was invalid seemed the simplest to me and matched the behavior of invalid ids being depth-only.",
      "parentUuid": "a14e9c33_daae2c2c",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1596,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 1607,
        "endChar": 5
      },
      "revId": "9376256c561aac3d5083454982dc17b5d4b681bd",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
